2.5. Carbonization of core–shell ZIF-8 $@$ ZIF-67 crystals. Core–shell ZIF-8@ZIF-67 crystals with micrometer-size were thermally converted to $\mathsf{N C@G C}$ nanocomposites through carbonization under a $\Nu_{2}$ flow at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 hours, with a heating rate of $2\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . After that, the obtained samples were washed extensively by HF solution $(10~\mathrm{wt\%})$ ) to remove the deposited $Z\mathrm{n}$ and Co species. As a control experiment, two types of NPCs were prepared by single precursors (ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 with micrometer-size) under the same thermal conditions, which are noted as NC and GC, respectively.
2.6. Electrochemical measurements. The electrochemical measurements were conducted in a standard three-electrode electrochemical cell. Platinum and $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl}$ (3 M KCl) electrodes were selected as the counter electrode and reference electrode, respectively. The working electrode was prepared according to the following process. One mg of NC@GC composites derived from core–shell ZIF-8 $@$ ZIF-67 crystals was mixed with poly(vinylidine difluoride) $\mathrm{(0.1~mg)}$ and then dissolved in N-methylpyrrolidinone solvent (0.5 mL). The obtained slurry was homogenized by ultrasonication, dropped onto graphite substrates $(1~\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ ), and dried under infrared lamp to form the thin film on the electrodes. For comparison, nanoporous NC and GC materials were prepared from single ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 crystals, respectively. The electrochemical measurements were carried out using an electrochemical workstation (CHI 660E CH Instruments) in acid electrolyte $\mathrm{1.0~M~H_{2}S O_{4}},$ ). The electrochemical properties of the supercapacitor were investigated by cyclic voltammetry (CV) and galvanostatic charge–discharge measurements. The charge–discharge current density varied from 2 to $20\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The specific capacitance was calculated from the galvanostatic charge-discharge curves using the following equation, C = ×∆ , where $I$ is charge-discharge current at a discharge time ∆t (s), ∆V is the potential range, and m is the mass of active electrode materials. 2.7. Characterization. The morphology of the samples was characterized using a Hitachi SU-8000 fieldemission scanning electron microscope (SEM) at an accelerating voltage of $5~\mathrm{kV}.$ Transmission electron microscope (TEM) and elemental mapping analysis were measured by a JEM-2100 operated at $200~\mathrm{kV}.$ Wide-angle powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns were measured by a Rigaku Rint 2000 X-ray diffractometer using monochromated Cu Kα radiation ( $40\ \mathrm{kV}