The porosity of ZIF-8, ZIF-67, and core–shell ZIF-8 $@$ ZIF-67(0.26) crystals was measured by $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms. As shown in Fig. S5a, all of the samples displayed type I isotherms with steep $\Nu_{2}$ uptakes at low relative pressure, which is typically associated with microporosity. The BrunauerEmmett-Teller (BET) surface areas (SBET), Langmuir surface area (SLangmuir), total pore volumes $(V_{\mathrm{pore}})_{:}$ , and pore sizes are summarized in Table S2. It is observable that the pore sizes for all three samples are the same. The surface area and the pore volume of ZIF-8@ZIF-67(0.26) crystals increased almost $10\%$ in comparison to those of ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 crystals. Thus, there is no serious pore blocking at the interface between core ZIF-8 and shell ZIF-67 crystals. As clearly seen in wide-angle PXRD results (Fig. 2c), the crystal structure and its lattice constant of ZIF-8 (core) are almost the same as those of ZIF-67 (shell). Therefore, the ZIF-67 shell can grow well on the surface of ZIF-8 crystals. As far as we know, the seed-mediated growth method reported here, for the first time, realized the preparation of novel core–shell ZIF-8 $@$ ZIF-67 crystals with controllable core sizes and adjustable shell thicknesses at the nanometer scale.
