The surface areas and pore-size distributions were investigated by $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms (Fig. 4c-d). The sharp uptakes at low relative pressure $(<~0.05)$ indicated the presence of micropores. The hysteresis loops and the gradual uptakes at a relative pressure range from 0.45 to 1.0, which were caused by the capillary condensation of $\Nu_{2}$ in the mesopores with wide size distributions, were more distinct in the GC and NC@GC samples. Such hysteresis loops have often been observed in large mesoporous materials (e.g., SBA-type mesoporous silica).19 Thus, the interconnected hierarchically micro/mesoporous structures were well-developed, as confirmed by the pore size distributions for each sample. The mesopore sizes in GC and ${\mathsf{N C@G C}}$ were mainly generated by the carbonization process and the subsequent removal of Co nanoparticles with relatively small size $3{\cdot}4~\mathrm{nm}\dot{}$ ). Therefore, the mesopores are not uniform. The surface area $(S_{\mathrm{BET}})$ and pore volume ( $\lvert V_{\mathrm{pore}})$ for the samples are summarized in Table 1. The NC sample possessed a high surface area of $1499\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1},$ , which was a bit lower than that of the parent ZIF-8 crystals $(1727\mathrm{~m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}).$ However, the surface area of the GC sample with a highly graphitic structure was dramatically decreased to $496\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The value is extremely low in comparison to the original ZIF-67 crystals $(1738\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ due to the collapse of the well-defined microporous structure of ZIF-67 caused by the graphitization of amorphous carbon.10d,11c Furthermore, the ratio of the microporous surface area $(S_{\mathrm{{micro}}}/S_{\mathrm{{BET}}})$ in NC $(25.2\%)$ is much higher than that in GC $(10.0\%)$ , confirming the presence of a large amount of mesopores in GC. As we expected, the surface areas of the $\mathsf{N C@G C}$ samples were the intermediate value between NC and GC. The NC@GC(0.05) sample with a thin GC shell showed a higher surface area $(1276~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ than the $\mathsf{N C}@\mathrm{GC}(0.35)$ sample with a thick GC shell $(813~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , which also indirectly proves a successful tuning of the GC shell thickness.
